    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-275759",
      "knowledge_point_id": "add52a4e-fff5-5cf6-8fe9-85a44063e2a8",
      "knowledge_point": "Rotating a Vector About the Origin",
      "problem": "The vector $v=⟨3,-5⟩$ is rotated ${45}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the origin. Find the image of $v$ under this rotation.\n\n${v}^{′}=⟨$ \n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}}\n$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "A rotation of ${45}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* about the origin is represented by the matrix \n\n$$\n{R}_{({45}^{∘})}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image ${v}^{′}$ under the action of the matrix ${R}_{({45}^{∘})}$, we compute the matrix product, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{′} & ={R}_{({45}^{∘})}\\cdot v \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image is ${v}^{′}=⟨4\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}⟩.$",
