      "worked_solution": "A rotation of ${45}^{∘}$ counterclockwise about the origin is represented by the matrix \n\n$$\n{R}_{{45}^{∘}}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image ${v}^{′}$ under the action of the matrix ${R}_{{45}^{∘}}$, we compute the matrix product, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{′} & ={R}_{{45}^{∘}}\\cdot v \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image is ${v}^{′}=⟨\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2}⟩.$",
