    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-82050",
      "knowledge_point_id": "bea036e3-19cd-572b-a6cb-2fa466c2a399",
      "knowledge_point": "Interpreting a Given Matrix as a Rotation",
      "problem": "What is the geometrical interpretation of the transformation represented by the matrix $A$ given below? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\n{R}_{\\theta }=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ are positive, we must have $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians anticlockwise about the origin.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians clockwise about the origin."
