    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-8475",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b2371a64-5e91-5caa-91c0-f6c7ff572c70",
      "knowledge_point": "Rotating an Object About the Origin",
      "problem": "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under a **clockwise** rotation of ${135}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$",
      "worked_solution": "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =-{135}^{∘}$ into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{135}^{∘}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{135}^{∘}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{135}^{∘}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{135}^{∘})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the coordinates of ${S}^{′}$ are\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(0,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
